  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1022 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nWV imagery showing main upper low now over western Nevada. Low will \ncontinue lifting out northward as a secondary low eventually closes \noff over SoCal and Arizona. Leading trough from the low extends N-S \nalong the AZ/NM state line with good drying behind it.\nThis trough delineates the western edge of this evening's \nconvection. A weakening surface front from western Arizona down \nacross the Baja will continue east possibly near the AZ/NM before \nwashing out. The upper trough will continue to slowly drifting east \nover central New Mexico Friday afternoon before washing out. This \nwill usher drier air into the western CWA (PWs of around .6 inches \ncompared to 1.0 inch in the east). Decent CAPE values will continue \nFriday, along with some speed shear. Thus expect scattered \nconvection again from about Deming east, with more isolated storms \nto the west, though some severe storms still possible either side of \nthis line. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...upper low continues to slowly lift northward \nfrom Arizona up to southern Utah. This will push deeper layered \nmoisture east of the RG Valley, where decent chances of storms will \ncontinue. Expect best storm coverage from the RG Valley, with just \nisolated showers/storms to the west. Lowering PWs all areas suggest \nless chance of heavy rain/flooding. Some severe storm potential will \ncontinue but should be limited mostly to the RG Valley east. \n\nMonday and Tuesday...main upper low exits into the central Plains \nwith drier air aloft (at least at upper levels). GFS shows mid-level \nwinds (700mb) remaining southerly and keeping some shallower \nmoisture in the eastern CWA, with a slight chance of showers Monday \nfrom El Paso east. Moisture clears out Tuesday as mid level winds \nbriefly turn more southwest. \n\nWednesday and Thursday...GFS remains bullish in bringing up more \nmoisture with the help of a Pacific short wave approaching the area. \nThus GFS has west two days here while the ECMWF remains mostly dry.",
